<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Commune of Treviso</h1><p class="st-meta">1164 CE – 1339 CE (approx.) · capital Treviso</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The city-republic at the heart of the 'joyful March', whose courtly culture under the da Camino lords was praised by Dante.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Treviso, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, had a commune of consuls in the 12th century and joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/veronese-league">Veronese League</a> in 1164 and then the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lombard-league">Lombard League</a>. It dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-of-treviso">March of Treviso</a>, a region of rivers and fertile plain between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and the lagoon.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The commune was governed by consuls and later a podestà. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezzelino-iii-da-romano">Ezzelino III da Romano's</a> brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberico-da-romano">Alberico</a> it suffered harsh rule until 1259. The da Camino family then dominated; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gherardo-da-camino">Gherardo da Camino</a>, lord from 1283 to 1306, kept a brilliant court that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> praised, and the region's festive life earned it the name Marca gioiosa. Treviso traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and the Alpine valleys.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cangrande-i-della-scala">Cangrande della Scala</a> took Treviso in 1329, joining it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-verona">Lordship of Verona</a>. In 1339 it submitted to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice</a>, its first major mainland possession, which held it until 1797.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 1164 CE – 1339 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Italy</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:82.87%;width:3.48%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1164 CE – 1339 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Treviso</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gherardo III da Camino, Rizzardo IV da Camino</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Venetian, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Treviso, Marca Trevigiana</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1252&amp;focus=commune-of-treviso" aria-label="Commune of Treviso on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4970 568 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Commune of Treviso lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5290 824L5293 831"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5293 831h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5290 824h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5290 824h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Treviso. <a href="/atlas?year=1252&amp;focus=commune-of-treviso">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

